prompt:
Draw the page exactly as a browser renders it from this source. The grid uses the browser's default auto-placement (grid-auto-flow: row, sparse) — work out for yourself which cells each panel ends up covering, and which cells stay empty.

```html
<div id="page">
  <h1 class="hd">Exhibition Floor</h1>
  <p class="sub">stand allocation grid</p>
  <main class="board">
    <div class="panel pA">A</div>
    <div class="panel pB">B</div>
    <div class="panel pC">C</div>
    <div class="panel pD">D</div>
    <div class="panel pE">E</div>
    <div class="panel pF">F</div>
    <div class="panel pG">G</div>
    <div class="panel pH">H</div>
  </main>
  <p class="foot">8 stands</p>
</div>
```

```css
.hd { margin:0 0 6px; font:bold 34px sans-serif; color:#1b1b1b }
.sub { margin:0 0 28px; font:20px sans-serif; color:#5a5a5a }
.foot { margin:28px 0 0; font:20px sans-serif; color:#5a5a5a }
.board { display:grid; grid-template-columns:repeat(6, 116px); grid-auto-rows:116px; gap:12px; padding:0; background:#00acc1 }
.panel { box-sizing:border-box; border:3px solid #4a4a4a; color:#111; display:flex; align-items:center; justify-content:center; font:bold 20px sans-serif }
.pA { background:#e53935; grid-column:span 2 }
.pB { background:#2e7d32; grid-row:span 2 }
.pC { background:#ffeb3b; grid-column:span 3 }
.pD { background:#8e24aa; grid-column:span 2 }
.pE { background:#e53935 }
.pF { background:#2e7d32; grid-column:span 2 }
.pG { background:#ffeb3b }
.pH { background:#8e24aa; grid-column:span 2 }
```

Output ONLY the rendered page as a clean, flat screenshot: the dark heading and small grey sub-heading exactly as the markup gives them, and below them the grid itself, which occupies exactly 3 rows of 6 equal columns. Every panel is filled with one flat solid colour and carries its dark letter; any grid cell no panel covers shows the container's own background colour. Show no source code, no browser window, address bar, tabs or developer tools, no legend, no colour names, no grid-line numbers, no arrows or annotations, no shadows or gradients. The page background outside the grid is plain white, and every coloured area in the image must be inside the grid rectangle.
